    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly, we need to determine the dimensions of the square and how the rectangles can be arranged to fit perfectly.\n\nFirst, let's denote the side length of the square as \\( s \\). The area of the square must be able to be expressed as the product of the number of rectangles and the area of each rectangle. The area of each 2-by-3 rectangle is \\( 2 \\times 3 = 6 \\) square units. Therefore, the area of the square is \\( 6k \\) where \\( k \\) is the number of rectangles.\n\nSince the side length of the square is \\( s \\), the area of the square is \\( s^2 \\). So, we need:\n\\[ s^2 = 6k \\]\nThis implies that \\( s^2 \\) must be a multiple of 6. Since 6 is \\( 2 \\times 3 \\), \\( s \\) must be a multiple of both 2 and 3, and hence a multiple of \\( \\text{lcm}(2, 3) = 6 \\). Therefore, \\( s \\) must be at least 6. Let's check if a square with side length 6 can be covered by 6 rectangles.\n\nIf \\( s = 6 \\), the area of the square is \\( 6^2 = 36 \\) square units. The area of each rectangle is 6 square units, so the number of rectangles needed is:\n\\[ k = \\frac{36}{6} = 6 \\]\nNow, we need to check if 6 rectangles, each a 2-by-3 rectangle, can indeed cover a 6-by-6 square. We can arrange the rectangles as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n1 & 1 & 2 & 2 & 3 & 3 \\\\\n\\hline\n4 & 4 & 5 & 5 & 6 & 6 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nHere, each number represents a rectangle, and each rectangle covers two squares. This arrangement uses exactly 6 rectangles to cover a 6-by-6 square, which is the smallest possible number of rectangles that can cover such a square neatly.\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
